 \n V2A-mapper: a lightweight solution for vision-to-audio generation by connecting foundation models .\n\n In AAAI ,\n\n pp. 15492–15501 .\n\n Cited by: Appendix F ,\n Main Results .\n\n J. Wang, Z. Chen, B. Yuan, K. Zheng, C. Li, Y. Jiang, and J. Zhu (2026) \n AudioMoG: guiding audio generation with mixture-of-guidance .\n\n In ICME ,\n\n Cited by: Annealed Temporal Differences Guidance .\n\n X. Wang, Y. Wang, Y. Wu, R. Song, X. Tan, Z. Chen, H. Xu, and G. Sui (2024b) \n TiVA: time-aligned video-to-audio generation .\n\n In ACM MM ,\n\n pp. 573–582 .\n\n Cited by: Appendix A ,\n Introduction ,\n Introduction ,\n Visual Representation in V2A Generation. ,\n Investigation of TD Representation ,\n Visual Content. .\n\n Y. Wang, W. Guo, R. Huang, J. Huang, Z. Wang, F. You, R. Li, and Z. Zhao (2024c) \n Frieren: efficient video-to-audio generation network with rectified flow matching .\n\n In NeurIPS ,\n\n pp. 128118–128138 .\n\n Cited by: Main Results .\n\n Y. Wang, Z. Chen, X. Chen, Y. Wei, J. Zhu, and J. Chen (2025) \n FrameBridge: improving image-to-video generation with bridge models .\n\n In ICML ,\n\n pp. 62596–62626 .\n\n Cited by: Introduction ,\n Motivation: TD Distinguish V2A from I2A .\n\n Y. Wu, K. Chen, T. Zhang, Y. Hui, T. Berg-Kirkpatrick, and S. Dubnov (2023) \n Large-scale contrastive language-audio pretraining with feature fusion and keyword-to-caption augmentation .\n\n In ICASSP ,\n\n pp. 1–5 .\n\n Cited by: Textual Semantics. .\n\n M. Xu, C. Li, Y. Ren, R. Chen, Y. Gu, W. Liang, and D. Yu (2024) \n Video-to-audio generation with hidden alignment .\n\n External Links: 2407.07464 \n\n Cited by: Appendix F ,\n Main Results .\n\n Y. Zhang, Y. Gu, Y. Zeng, Z. Xing, Y. Wang, Z. Wu, B. Liu, and K. Chen (2026) \n FoleyCrafter: bring silent videos to life with lifelike and synchronized sounds .\n\n IJCV 134 ( 1 ), pp. 46 .\n\n Cited by: Appendix F ,\n Datasets. ,\n Main Results .\n\n H. Zhao, C. Gan, A. Rouditchenko, C. Vondrick, J. McDermott, and A. Torralba (2018) \n The sound of pixels .\n\n In ECCV ,\n\n pp. 570–586 .\n\n Cited by: Introduction .\n\n Appendix A Audio Latent Diffusion Model \n\n Diffusion models have advanced cross-modal audio generation with the strong capability to faithfully capture the target distribution  (Ho et al. 2020 ; Song et al. 2021 ) .\nThey are composed of two processes: a  forward process transforming the data distribution into a known prior distribution,  e.g. , standard Gaussian distribution, and a  reverse process gradually reconstructing the target distribution from the prior distribution with iterative sampling steps.\nIn recent works, audio generation systems are usually built upon a latent diffusion framework.\nAt early stage, the small space is compressed from the mel-spectrogram and the entire generation process includes latent diffusion sampling, VAE decoding, and a vocoder transforming mel-spectrogram to waveform  (Liu et al. 2023 , 2024a ; Wang et al. 2024b ) . \n\n In this work, we directly develop the diffusion model in the latent space compressed from audio waveform, avoiding cascaded VAE and vocoder.\nThe details of VAE is introduced in Appendix  D .\nGiven an audio sample 𝒙 0 ∈ ℝ L \\bm{x}_{0}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L} sampled from the observed dataset, where L L is the sample length, audio generation systems usually develop a compression network to encode it into a latent representation 𝒛 0 ∈ ℝ T d × F d \\bm{z}_{0}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{T_{d}\\times F_{d}} , where T d T_{d} and F d F_{d} denote the temporal and feature dimension, respectively.\nThen, in the compressed latent space, a forward process of the latent diffusion model injects Gaussian noise ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ​ ( 𝟎 , 𝐈 ) \\bm{\\epsilon}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mathbf{0},\\mathbf{I}) into 𝒛 0 \\bm{z}_{0} with a predefined noise schedule β t \\beta_{t} , constructing noisy representations 𝒛 t ∈ ℝ T d × F d \\bm{z}_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{T_{d}\\times F_{d}} at each time step t ∈ ( 0 , T ] t\\in(0,T] with: \n\n q ​ ( 𝒛 t | 𝒛 0 ) = 𝒩 ​ ( 𝒛 t ; α ¯ t ​ 𝒛 0 , ( 1 − α ¯ t ) ​ 𝐈 ) , q(\\bm{z}_{t}|\\bm{z}_{0})=\\mathcal{N}(\\bm{z}_{t};\\sqrt{\\bar{\\alpha}_{t}}\\,\\bm{z}_{0},\\,(1-\\bar{\\alpha}_{t})\\mathbf{I}), \n\n (10) \n\n where α ¯ t = ∏ s = 1 t ( 1 − β s ) \\bar{\\alpha}_{t}=\\prod_{s=1}^{t}(1-\\beta_{s}) indicates the noise level at time step t t .\nWhen t = T t=T , the clean latent representation 𝒛 0 \\bm{z}_{0} at time step t = 0 t=0 will be transformed into uninformative Gaussian noise 𝒛 T \\bm{z}_{T} . \n\n In generation, the reverse process starts from Gaussian noise 𝒛 T \\bm{z}_{T} , gradually reconstructing the target 𝒛 0 \\bm{z}_{0} with an iterative denoising and refinement trajectory: \n\n p θ ​ ( 𝒛 t − 1 | 𝒛 t ) = 𝒩 ​ ( 𝒛 t − 1 ; 𝝁 θ ​ ( 𝒛 t , t ) , σ t ​ 𝐈 ) , p_{\\theta}(\\bm{z}_{t-1}|\\bm{z}_{t})=\\mathcal{N}\\big(\\bm{z}_{t-1};\\bm{\\mu}_{\\theta}(\\bm{z}_{t},t),\\,\\sigma_{t}\\mathbf{I}\\big), \n\n (11) \n\n where the time-dependent variation σ t \\sigma_{t} is usually predefined according to β t \\beta_{t} and the mean function 𝝁 θ ​ ( 𝒛 t , t ) \\bm{\\mu}_{\\theta}(\\bm{z}_{t},t) can be parameterized with different methods and predicted by a neural network.\nFor diffusion-based audio generation systems  (Liu et al. 2023 ; Evans et al. 2025 ) , noise prediction is a popular parameterization method, where the network is optimized with the objective: \n\n ℒ audio = 𝔼 𝒛 0 , ϵ , t ​ [ ‖ ϵ − ϵ θ ​ ( α ¯ t ​ 𝒛 0 + 1 − α ¯ t ​ ϵ , t ) ‖ 2 2 ] . \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{audio}}=\\mathbb{E}_{\\bm{z}_{0},\\bm{\\epsilon},t}\\Big[\\big\\|\\bm{\\epsilon}-\\bm{\\epsilon}_{\\theta}(\\sqrt{\\bar{\\alpha}_{t}}\\,\\bm{z}_{0}+\\sqrt{1-\\bar{\\alpha}_{t}}\\,\\bm{\\epsilon},t)\\big\\|_{2}^{", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "df7a3019-4d88-4db8-b2c6-83d2cb0093ca", "uuid": "a557f277-7f9a-498a-84c5-039c0ace0493", "timestamp": "2026-08-06T05:38:11.967Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": ". Gramaccioni, E. Postolache, E. Rodolà, D. Comminiello, and J. D. Reiss (2024) \n Syncfusion: m